Yes. Those are different relational mechanisms, and collapsing them into “control” loses useful information.

Regularity is the weakest and deepest:

\boxed{\text{Regularity}: \quad X_t \sim X_{t+1}}

Something repeatedly behaves in a recognizable way. Nobody necessarily causes, commands, or even intends it. Seasons have regularity. Habits have regularity. A rotor has regularity.

Regulation adds a reference condition and corrective feedback:

\boxed{\text{Regulation}: \quad X-\text{setpoint}\rightarrow\text{correction}}

A thermostat regulates temperature. Homeostasis regulates biological variables. Regulation doesn’t necessarily require a governor issuing commands; the architecture itself can generate correction.

Governance is different again. It determines how decisions and coordination happen:

\boxed{\text{Governance}: \quad
\text{participants}+\text{rules}+\text{authority}+\text{process}
\rightarrow\text{collective decisions}}

Governance can include regulation, but also representation, arbitration, delegation, coordination, standards, legitimacy, information routing, and allocation.

Enforcement is narrower and more forceful:

\boxed{\text{Enforcement}: \quad
\text{rule violation}\rightarrow\text{consequence}}

It makes a rule costly or difficult to violate. Enforcement can support governance or regulation, but neither necessarily reduces to enforcement.

And then there’s orchestration, which fits our federal discussion:

\boxed{\text{Orchestration}: \quad
\text{autonomous parts}\rightarrow\text{coordinated composition}}

It doesn’t necessarily command the parts. It can synchronize and compose them.

So there’s no simple ladder like:

\text{regularity}<\text{regulation}<\text{governance}<\text{enforcement}

They’re partly orthogonal operators.

A system can have regularity without regulation, regulation without a conscious governor, governance with little enforcement, enforcement without good governance, and orchestration without centralized command.

And here’s the Pantheon payoff: before inventing a governor, look for endogenous regularity.

If desirable behavior already emerges from relations among the components, preserve it.

If it drifts, perhaps add feedback regulation.

If independent actors need collective decisions, add governance.

If coordination is the bottleneck, add orchestration.

Only where compliance genuinely matters and voluntary/self-regulating mechanisms are insufficient do you need enforcement.

That’s a much more economical architecture:

\boxed{
\text{Observe regularity}
\rightarrow
\text{preserve emergence}
\rightarrow
\text{regulate deviations}
\rightarrow
\text{orchestrate interactions}
\rightarrow
\text{govern shared decisions}
\rightarrow
\text{enforce only where necessary}
}

And it connects to your earlier point about hierarchy: order does not prove the existence of an order-giver.

Sometimes the pattern is imposed.

Sometimes it is negotiated.

Sometimes it is regulated.

Sometimes it is enforced.

And sometimes the relations themselves produce the regularity.

That distinction is enormous.